.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::HTTP 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::HTTP
.Nd one HTTP/1.1 codec, for both ends of a connection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::HTTP;

# A server frames, then parses
while (1) {
	my $length = FuguLib::HTTP::message_complete($buffer,
	    max_size => 65536);
	last unless defined $length;	# over the limit, close
	last if $length == 0;		# read more

	my $message = substr $buffer, 0, $length, '';
	my $request = FuguLib::HTTP::parse_request($message);
	...
}

# and answers
print $sock FuguLib::HTTP::build_response(
    status  => 200,
    headers => { 'Content-Type' => 'application/hap+json' },
    body    => $json,
);

# A client builds and parses the other way round
my $bytes    = FuguLib::HTTP::build_request(method => 'GET', path => '/');
my $response = FuguLib::HTTP::parse_response($answer);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is functions over strings.
It opens no socket, holds no connection state, and never logs.
A server calls
.Fn parse_request
and
.Fn build_response ;
a client calls
.Fn build_request
and
.Fn parse_response .
The two ends thus read and write the same bytes, and no second parser
can drift from the first.
.Pp
The module knows nothing about any protocol built on HTTP.
A status code that belongs to one application, and the header
defaults that one application wants, are arguments.
.Ss message_complete
.Fn message_complete "$buffer" "%args"
reports how many bytes at the front of
.Fa $buffer
form one whole message.
.Pp
This is the framing, and a stream server needs it.
A socket gives a reader whatever arrived, which is not a message: a
request can span two reads, and two requests can share one.
A reader that parses each read as a whole message drops the second
request and mangles the split one.
The caller keeps a buffer and asks this function how much of it is a
message.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "max_sizeXX"
.It Fa max_size
Refuse a message larger than this.
The default is 65536 bytes.
.El
.Ss parse_request
.Fn parse_request "$data"
parses one request message.
The function returns a hash reference with
.Va method ,
.Va path ,
.Va version ,
.Va headers
and
.Va body .
.Pp
The header names are lowercase.
A peer chooses their case, and a caller must not depend on the choice.
A repeated header keeps its last value.
.Ss parse_response
.Fn parse_response "$data"
parses one response message.
The function returns a hash reference with
.Va status ,
.Va reason ,
.Va version ,
.Va headers
and
.Va body .
.Ss build_request
.Fn build_request "%args"
builds one request message.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "versionXX"
.It Fa method
The default is GET.
.It Fa path
The default is /.
.It Fa version
The default is 1.1.
.It Fa headers
A hash reference, sent in sorted order.
.It Fa body
The body bytes.
.El
.Pp
The function adds Content-Length when the caller gave a body and no
such header.
A peer cannot frame a body without it.
.Ss build_response
.Fn build_response "%args"
builds one response message.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "status_textXX"
.It Fa status
The default is 200.
.It Fa status_text
The default comes from the code.
.It Fa version
The default is 1.1.
.It Fa headers
A hash reference, sent in sorted order.
.It Fa body
The body bytes.
.El
.Pp
The function always adds Content-Length.
A response with no length and no close is a response that the peer
waits out.
.Pp
The header order is stable.
Two calls with the same arguments give the same bytes, which a
conformance test needs.
.Ss status_text
.Fn status_text "$code"
returns the reason phrase of a status code.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn message_complete
returns the length of the message, 0 when more bytes are necessary,
and
.Dv undef
when the message is over the limit.
.Pp
.Fn parse_request
and
.Fn parse_response
return a hash reference, or
.Dv undef
when the first line is not a request line or a status line.
.Pp
.Fn build_request
and
.Fn build_response
return the message bytes.
.Fn status_text
returns a string, and
.Sq Unknown
for a code it does not hold.
.Sh EXAMPLES
An application with a status code of its own passes the text:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
FuguLib::HTTP::build_response(
    status      => 470,
    status_text => 'Connection Authorization Required',
);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
No function dies.
A malformed message gives
.Dv undef ,
and the caller decides what to answer.
.Pp
A caller that gets
.Dv undef
from
.Fn message_complete
closes the connection.
The peer is either broken or hostile, and no further byte can make the
message valid.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr FuguLib::JSONSocket 3p ,
.Xr FuguLib::Proxy 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The module frames by Content-Length only.
It does not do chunked transfer encoding, and it does not do
compression.
A peer that sends either is a peer this codec cannot read.
.Pp
.Fn message_complete
reads the declared length, not the bytes that arrived.
A peer that declares more than it sends holds a slot until the caller
times it out.
An event loop, not this module, owns that deadline.
